{% callout type="summary" %}
**Quick answer:** The Krebs cycle (TCA cycle) is a series of reactions in the mitochondrial matrix that oxidises the acetyl part of acetyl CoA to two CO2 and reduces coenzymes. Acetyl CoA joins oxaloacetate to make citrate, and oxaloacetate is regenerated. Each turn gives 3 NADH, 1 FADH2 and 1 GTP, and the cycle runs only in aerobic conditions.
{% /callout %}

{% cbmeTags codes="BC3.3" phase="Phase 1" /%}

{% figure src="/learn-images/biochemistry/krebs-cycle/krebs-cycle-overview-carbon-count.webp" alt="Circular diagram of the Krebs cycle showing acetyl CoA entering, two CO2 leaving, and NADH, FADH2 and GTP produced." caption="The Krebs cycle at a glance: acetyl CoA goes in, two CO2 come out, and reduced coenzymes are made." credit="en:User:TotoBaggins, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY-SA 3.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Citricacidcycle_ball.png" width="960" height="640" /%}

## What is the Krebs cycle?

The Krebs cycle is also called the **citric acid cycle** or the **tricarboxylic acid (TCA) cycle**. It is a sequence of reactions in mitochondria. It oxidises the acetyl part of **acetyl CoA** (a two-carbon unit carried on coenzyme A) to CO2 and reduces coenzymes. The respiratory chain then reoxidises those coenzymes, and this is linked to the formation of ATP.

The name "TCA" is used because tricarboxylic acids (citrate, cis-aconitate and isocitrate) take part at the start of the cycle. The cycle was proposed by Hans Adolf Krebs in 1937 and is named in his honour.

Why it matters:

- It is the **final common oxidative pathway** for carbohydrates, fats and amino acids. Glucose, fatty acids and most amino acids are broken down to acetyl CoA or to intermediates of the cycle.
- It is described as the most important pathway for the energy supply of the body. About 65 to 70% of the ATP is made through it, and it uses about two-thirds of the oxygen the body consumes.
- It also supplies intermediates for making amino acids, glucose and heme.

## Where does it happen?

The enzymes of the cycle are in the **mitochondrial matrix**, next to the electron transport chain (ETC). This allows ATP to be made by oxidative phosphorylation without any hindrance.

One enzyme is different. **Succinate dehydrogenase** is the only enzyme of the cycle that is embedded in the inner mitochondrial membrane. There it works as **Complex II** of the ETC.

Not all cells have mitochondria. Red blood cells have none, so they cannot run the cycle, and lactate production goes on at a baseline level all the time in the body.

## The big picture

The cycle begins when a two-carbon **acetyl CoA** combines with a four-carbon **oxaloacetate** to form a six-carbon tricarboxylic acid, **citrate**. In the reactions that follow, two carbons leave as CO2 and oxaloacetate is regenerated.

Oxaloacetate is not used up, so it is said to play a **catalytic role**. It can combine with another acetyl CoA and the cycle goes on.

The cycle is cyclic, but it is not a closed circle. Many compounds enter and leave it, like a heavy traffic circle on a highway with many connecting roads. Each intermediate that links to another pathway is one of those roads.

**Link reaction:** pyruvate is converted to acetyl CoA by the **pyruvate dehydrogenase (PDH) complex**. This step connects glycolysis to the cycle. Some authors describe it together with the cycle, but the Krebs cycle proper starts with the condensation of acetyl CoA and oxaloacetate.

## Steps of the cycle

{% figure src="/learn-images/biochemistry/krebs-cycle/krebs-cycle-enzymes-structures.webp" alt="Detailed diagram of the citric acid cycle with chemical structures, enzyme names, and the steps that release CO2 and make NADH, FADH2 and GTP." caption="Step by step: the ten reactions of the cycle, with the enzyme at each step and where coenzymes are reduced." credit="Theislikerice, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY-SA 4.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Citric_acid_cycle.png" width="960" height="890" /%}

{% table %}
- **Step**
- **Enzyme**
- **What happens**
- **Products and notes**
---
- **1**
- Citrate synthase
- Acetyl CoA condenses with oxaloacetate
- Citrate (6 carbons). Equilibrium lies far towards citrate.
---
- **2 and 3**
- Aconitase
- Citrate is isomerised to isocitrate in two stages: dehydration, then hydration, through cis-aconitate
- Isocitrate. Aconitase is an Fe-S protein.
---
- **4 and 5**
- Isocitrate dehydrogenase
- Oxidative decarboxylation: isocitrate goes to oxalosuccinate (enzyme bound), then to α-ketoglutarate
- **NADH and CO2** (first). Needs Mg2+ or Mn2+.
---
- **6**
- α-Ketoglutarate dehydrogenase complex
- Oxidative decarboxylation of α-ketoglutarate
- Succinyl CoA, **NADH and CO2** (second). Five cofactors: TPP, lipoamide, NAD+, FAD and CoA.
---
- **7**
- Succinate thiokinase (succinyl CoA synthetase)
- Cleaves the high-energy thioester bond of succinyl CoA, coupled to GDP going to GTP
- Succinate and **GTP**. Substrate-level phosphorylation. Nucleoside diphosphate kinase converts GTP to ATP.
---
- **8**
- Succinate dehydrogenase
- Succinate is oxidised to fumarate
- **FADH2** (not NADH)
---
- **9**
- Fumarase (fumarate hydratase)
- Adds water across the double bond of fumarate
- Malate
---
- **10**
- Malate dehydrogenase
- Malate is oxidised to oxaloacetate
- **NADH**. Oxaloacetate is regenerated.
{% /table %}

Points worth remembering about the steps:

- **Step 6 is like the PDH reaction.** The α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase complex needs the same cofactors as the PDH complex. Its equilibrium favours succinyl CoA so strongly that the reaction is physiologically one-way.
- **Step 7 is the only substrate-level phosphorylation** in the cycle. This means ATP or GTP is made directly from a high-energy bond, not through the respiratory chain.
- **Step 8 uses FAD, not NAD+**, because the reducing power of succinate is not enough to reduce NAD+. Succinate dehydrogenase contains FAD and iron-sulfur protein, and it reduces ubiquinone in the ETC directly.
- **Step 10:** the equilibrium of the malate dehydrogenase reaction strongly favours malate. The net flow still goes towards oxaloacetate, because oxaloacetate is continually removed (to form citrate, for gluconeogenesis, or by transamination to aspartate) and NADH is continually reoxidised.
- **Isocitrate dehydrogenase has three isoenzymes.** One uses NAD+ and is found only in mitochondria. The other two use NADP+ and are found in both mitochondria and cytosol. The NAD+-linked enzyme is the one linked to the respiratory chain.

**Summary equation for one turn:**

Acetyl CoA + 3 NAD+ + FAD + GDP + Pi + 2 H2O → 2 CO2 + 3 NADH + 3 H+ + FADH2 + GTP + CoA

{% callout type="tip" %}
**What one turn gives:** 2 CO2, 3 NADH, 1 FADH2 and 1 GTP. That is four reduced coenzymes per acetyl CoA. The NADH steps are isocitrate dehydrogenase, α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase and malate dehydrogenase. The FADH2 step is succinate dehydrogenase. The GTP step is succinate thiokinase.
{% /callout %}

## Why the cycle needs oxygen

Oxygen does not take part directly in the Krebs cycle. Even so, the cycle works **only under aerobic conditions**. The NAD+ and FAD it needs can be regenerated from NADH and FADH2 in the ETC only when O2 is present. So the cycle is strictly aerobic, while glycolysis works with or without oxygen.

## Energy yield

The reduced coenzymes pass into the respiratory chain. The number of ATP depends on which values a book uses:

{% figure src="/learn-images/biochemistry/krebs-cycle/etc-complexes-nadh-fadh2-entry.webp" alt="Diagram of the electron transport chain in the inner mitochondrial membrane showing complexes I to IV, the proton gradient and ATP synthase." caption="NADH and FADH2 from the cycle pass electrons down the respiratory chain, and the proton gradient drives ATP synthase." credit="OpenStax College, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY 3.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2508_The_Electron_Transport_Chain.jpg" width="960" height="660" /%}

{% table %}
- **Source (per acetyl CoA)**
- **Older values**
- **Newer values**
---
- **3 NADH**
- 9 ATP (3 each)
- About 7.5 ATP (about 2.5 each)
---
- **1 FADH2**
- 2 ATP
- About 1.5 ATP
---
- **1 GTP (substrate-level)**
- 1 ATP
- 1 ATP
---
- **Total**
- **12 ATP**
- **About 10 ATP**
{% /table %}

The PDH step before the cycle also makes one NADH. With the older values, the full oxidation of one pyruvate to CO2 gives 14 ATP from the reduced coenzymes plus 1 from substrate-level phosphorylation, which is 15 ATP in all.

## B vitamins in the cycle

Four B vitamins are essential for the cycle, and so for energy-yielding metabolism:

- **Thiamin (B1)**, as thiamin diphosphate (TPP): coenzyme for the decarboxylation in the α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase reaction
- **Riboflavin**, as FAD: cofactor for succinate dehydrogenase
- **Niacin**, as NAD+: electron acceptor for isocitrate dehydrogenase, α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase and malate dehydrogenase
- **Pantothenic acid**, as part of coenzyme A: forms acetyl CoA and succinyl CoA by esterifying carboxylic acids

Deficiency of thiamine or niacin can cause serious central nervous system problems, because brain cells cannot make enough ATP if the PDH complex is inactive. **Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome**, an encephalopathy-psychosis syndrome caused by thiamine deficiency, may be seen with alcohol abuse.

## Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex (the link step)

Pyruvate is decarboxylated and oxidised to acetyl CoA, with release of CO2 and formation of NADH:

{% figure src="/learn-images/biochemistry/krebs-cycle/pdh-complex-link-reaction.webp" alt="Diagram of the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex showing enzymes E1, E2 and E3 converting pyruvate to acetyl CoA, CO2 and NADH." caption="The PDH complex links glycolysis to the Krebs cycle by making acetyl CoA from pyruvate." credit="TstackPC, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY-SA 4.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pyruvate_Dehydrogenase_Mechanism.png" width="960" height="632" /%}

Pyruvate + NAD+ + CoA → Acetyl CoA + CO2 + NADH + H+

The PDH complex has three enzymes and five coenzymes:

- **E1**, pyruvate dehydrogenase (a decarboxylase): needs TPP
- **E2**, dihydrolipoyl transacetylase: needs lipoic acid and CoA
- **E3**, dihydrolipoyl dehydrogenase: needs FAD and NAD+

The complex also holds two tightly bound regulatory enzymes, PDH kinase and PDH phosphatase. Because the enzymes are physically associated, the reactions go in sequence without release of intermediates.

## Regulation

In most tissues the main job of the cycle is to yield energy. So **respiratory control** regulates it. Activity depends directly on the supply of NAD+. Because oxidation and phosphorylation are tightly coupled, NAD+ depends on the availability of ADP, and ultimately on how much ATP is being used for work.

On top of that, individual enzymes are regulated:

- The main sites are the non-equilibrium reactions of **pyruvate dehydrogenase, citrate synthase, isocitrate dehydrogenase and α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase**. Citrate synthase, isocitrate dehydrogenase and the α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase complex are the regulated enzymes with highly negative standard free energy change.
- **Citrate synthase** is not an allosteric enzyme in humans. It is inhibited by its product, citrate, and it also depends on substrate availability. Binding of oxaloacetate changes the enzyme so that a binding site for acetyl CoA forms.
- **α-Ketoglutarate dehydrogenase complex** is inhibited by its products, NADH and succinyl CoA, and activated by Ca2+. Unlike the PDH complex, it is not regulated by phosphorylation.
- **PDH complex:** phosphorylation by PDH kinase inactivates it. PDH kinase is activated by ATP, acetyl CoA and NADH, and inhibited by pyruvate. PDH phosphatase is activated by Ca2+.
- **Ca2+** activates the dehydrogenases. Its concentration rises during muscle contraction and during secretion in other tissues, when energy demand goes up.
- Both the PDH complex and the cycle are upregulated when ADP rises.
- In a tissue such as the brain, which depends largely on carbohydrate for acetyl CoA, control may occur at pyruvate dehydrogenase.

Unlike glycolysis, which is controlled mainly by phosphofructokinase, the cycle is controlled at several enzymes.

## Inhibitors of the cycle

{% table %}
- **Enzyme**
- **Inhibitor**
- **Type and notes**
---
- **Aconitase**
- Fluoroacetate
- Non-competitive. A **suicide substrate**: fluoroacetate is harmless itself, but is converted to fluoroacetyl CoA, which condenses with oxaloacetate to form fluorocitrate. Fluorocitrate inhibits aconitase and citrate accumulates.
---
- **α-Ketoglutarate dehydrogenase**
- Arsenite
- Non-competitive. α-Ketoglutarate accumulates.
---
- **Succinate dehydrogenase**
- Malonate
- Competitive
{% /table %}

Fluoroacetate is used as a rat poison. It is found in some plants, and eating them can be fatal to grazing animals. Some fluorinated anticancer agents and industrial chemicals, including pesticides, are metabolised to fluoroacetate.

## The cycle is amphibolic

The cycle is **amphibolic**: it works in both oxidative (catabolic) and synthetic (anabolic) processes. Besides oxidising acetyl CoA, it supplies carbon skeletons for other pathways:

- **Oxaloacetate** and **α-ketoglutarate** are precursors of aspartate and glutamate. These are needed to make other non-essential amino acids, purines and pyrimidines. Oxaloacetate is also used for gluconeogenesis.
- **Succinyl CoA** is used to make porphyrins and heme.
- **Citrate** is carried from the mitochondrion to the cytosol and cleaved to give acetyl CoA for the synthesis of fatty acids and sterols.

Amino acids also feed in. Glucogenic amino acids can give glucose through pyruvate or cycle intermediates. Ketogenic amino acids are precursors of acetyl CoA.

### Citrate and fatty acid synthesis

Fatty acid synthesis is a cytosolic pathway, but pyruvate dehydrogenase works inside the mitochondrion, and the mitochondrial membrane does not let acetyl CoA through. So citrate carries the acetyl groups out, and citrate lyase cleaves it in the cytosol.

Citrate leaves only when aconitase is saturated with its substrate, so citrate cannot pass straight from citrate synthase to aconitase. This makes sure citrate goes to fatty acid synthesis only when there is enough to keep the cycle running.

The oxaloacetate left behind cannot re-enter the mitochondrion. It is reduced to malate, which is oxidatively decarboxylated to pyruvate by the **malic enzyme**, reducing NADP+ to NADPH. This is the source of half the NADPH needed for fatty acid synthesis. The rest comes from the pentose phosphate pathway. Pyruvate then re-enters the mitochondrion and is carboxylated to oxaloacetate.

Citrate also inhibits phosphofructokinase, the rate-limiting enzyme of glycolysis, and activates acetyl CoA carboxylase, the rate-limiting enzyme of fatty acid synthesis.

### Anaplerosis

Drawing out intermediates for synthesis depletes the cycle, and it stops unless they are replaced. The reactions that refill the intermediates are called **anaplerotic reactions** (anaplerosis, from the Greek for "fill up"). Two examples:

- **Pyruvate carboxylase** converts pyruvate to oxaloacetate. It is an ATP-dependent carboxylation, and its coenzyme is the vitamin **biotin**.
- **Malic enzyme** converts pyruvate to malate, using NADP+.

Succinyl CoA can also be refilled from propionyl CoA, which comes from fatty acids with an odd number of carbons, and from the metabolism of several amino acids.

## Clinical correlations

**Hyperammonemia and coma.** In advanced liver disease and some rare genetic diseases of amino acid metabolism, ammonia rises. This causes loss of consciousness, coma and convulsions, and may be fatal. Symptoms include tremor, slurred speech and blurred vision. The mechanism:

1. Ammonia reacts with α-ketoglutarate to form glutamate (glutamate dehydrogenase) and then glutamine (glutamine synthetase).
2. Withdrawing α-ketoglutarate lowers the concentration of all the cycle intermediates.
3. In addition, ammonia inhibits α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, and possibly pyruvate dehydrogenase.
4. The cycle works less well, so brain ATP formation falls.

When plasma ammonia is very high, sodium benzoate and phenyllactate are given intravenously. They condense with glycine and glutamate to form water-soluble products that are easily excreted. Haemodialysis may sometimes be needed.

**PDH deficiency.** Deficiency of the PDH complex is the most common biochemical cause of congenital lactic acidosis. Most patients have a defect in the E1α subunit, with a gene mutation on the X chromosome and a male predominance. In the newborn it can cause hypotonia, convulsions, episodic apnoea, weak sucking, failure to thrive and coma. Congenital lactic acidosis can also be due to deficiency of dihydrolipoyl dehydrogenase, α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase or fumarase.

**Pyruvate carboxylase deficiency.** This interferes with the synthesis of oxaloacetate. Oxaloacetate depletion limits the cycle, and aspartate depletion impairs the urea cycle. Features include hypotonia, psychomotor retardation, failure to thrive, seizures and metabolic acidosis. Ketoacidosis results from the accumulation of acetyl CoA. Laboratory findings include raised blood lactate, pyruvate and alanine. Clinical severity follows the amount of residual enzyme activity.

**Genetic defects of cycle enzymes.** The few that have been reported are linked to severe neurological damage, because ATP formation in the central nervous system is very considerably impaired.

**Liver damage.** Liver is the only tissue in which gluconeogenesis, lipogenesis and amino acid interconversion all occur to a significant extent. The effects are therefore profound when many liver cells are damaged, as in acute hepatitis, or replaced by connective tissue, as in cirrhosis.

## Practice MCQs

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="Succinate dehydrogenase is the only enzyme of the cycle embedded in the inner mitochondrial membrane, where it works as Complex II of the electron transport chain. The other enzymes are in the matrix." %}
Which enzyme of the Krebs cycle is embedded in the inner mitochondrial membrane and also acts as Complex II of the electron transport chain?
- A. Isocitrate dehydrogenase
- B. Malate dehydrogenase
- C. Succinate dehydrogenase
- D. Fumarase
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="Oxygen is not used directly in the cycle. But NAD+ and FAD can be regenerated from NADH and FADH2 in the electron transport chain only when O2 is present, so the cycle stops without oxygen." %}
The Krebs cycle is strictly aerobic even though oxygen does not take part in any of its reactions. This is because:
- A. Citrate synthase needs oxygen as a substrate
- B. NAD+ and FAD are regenerated in the electron transport chain only in the presence of O2
- C. The cycle occurs in the cytosol, where oxygen is abundant
- D. Oxygen is needed to convert acetyl CoA to oxaloacetate
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="D" explanation="Ammonia withdraws α-ketoglutarate to form glutamate and then glutamine. This lowers the concentration of the cycle intermediates, and ammonia also inhibits α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase. Less ATP is formed in the brain, which leads to coma." %}
Which of the following best explains loss of consciousness in hyperammonemia?
- A. Excess ATP formation in neurons
- B. Direct activation of citrate synthase by ammonia
- C. Accumulation of fluorocitrate in the brain
- D. Withdrawal of α-ketoglutarate for glutamate formation, depleting cycle intermediates and lowering ATP
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="A" explanation="Fluoroacetate is converted to fluoroacetyl CoA, which condenses with oxaloacetate to form fluorocitrate. Fluorocitrate inhibits aconitase, so citrate accumulates." %}
Poisoning with fluoroacetate leads to accumulation of citrate because it inhibits:
- A. Aconitase
- B. Citrate synthase
- C. Succinate dehydrogenase
- D. Malate dehydrogenase
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="Succinate thiokinase (succinyl CoA synthetase) cleaves the thioester bond of succinyl CoA and couples this to GDP going to GTP. This is the only substrate-level phosphorylation in the cycle." %}
The only step of the Krebs cycle that gives a high-energy phosphate directly (substrate-level phosphorylation) is catalysed by:
- A. Isocitrate dehydrogenase
- B. α-Ketoglutarate dehydrogenase
- C. Succinate thiokinase
- D. Fumarase
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="Intermediates drawn out for synthesis must be replaced or the cycle stops. Pyruvate carboxylase makes oxaloacetate from pyruvate and is an anaplerotic reaction. It is ATP-dependent and needs biotin." %}
Which reaction is an example of an anaplerotic reaction of the Krebs cycle?
- A. Conversion of citrate to isocitrate by aconitase
- B. Conversion of pyruvate to oxaloacetate by pyruvate carboxylase
- C. Conversion of succinate to fumarate by succinate dehydrogenase
- D. Conversion of malate to oxaloacetate by malate dehydrogenase
{% /mcq %}

## Viva questions

{% viva question="What is the Krebs cycle and where does it occur?" %}
A sequence of reactions that oxidises the acetyl part of acetyl CoA to CO2 and reduces coenzymes, which are reoxidised in the respiratory chain with formation of ATP. It occurs in the mitochondrial matrix, next to the electron transport chain.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Why is the Krebs cycle called amphibolic?" %}
It works in both oxidative and synthetic processes. Besides oxidising acetyl CoA, it supplies carbon skeletons for gluconeogenesis, acetyl CoA for fatty acid synthesis (through citrate), and intermediates for amino acids and heme.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="How much ATP is formed from one acetyl CoA?" %}
Older values give 12 ATP: 9 from 3 NADH, 2 from FADH2 and 1 from substrate-level phosphorylation. Newer values give about 10 ATP.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Which B vitamins are needed for the cycle?" %}
Thiamin (TPP, for α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase), riboflavin (FAD, for succinate dehydrogenase), niacin (NAD+, for isocitrate, α-ketoglutarate and malate dehydrogenases) and pantothenic acid (coenzyme A, in acetyl CoA and succinyl CoA).
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Name three inhibitors of the Krebs cycle and their sites." %}
Fluoroacetate inhibits aconitase (through fluorocitrate), arsenite inhibits α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, and malonate competitively inhibits succinate dehydrogenase.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Why is ammonia toxic to the brain?" %}
It reacts with α-ketoglutarate to form glutamate. This depletes cycle intermediates, and ammonia also inhibits α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase. Brain ATP formation falls, causing coma and convulsions.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What are anaplerotic reactions? Give an example." %}
Reactions that refill cycle intermediates drawn out for synthesis. Example: pyruvate carboxylase converting pyruvate to oxaloacetate, using ATP and biotin.
{% /viva %}

